239:Follow-up EXP-G02 (RULINGS Q32b): QLever in-range companion vectors by an independent golden author. For every golden vector (or vector case) that commits an integer outside QLever's accepted range, the evaluator applies the compiled lane outcome in LEGACY_LANE_OUTCOMES instead of the vector's single expected answer: XTDB commits and keeps the original answer; QLever's qualified write routes reject the command with E-VALUE-OUT-OF-RANGE before any mutation (RULINGS Q23, Q31j). The original vectors stay unchanged as audit evidence (RULINGS Q29a). QLever's in-range coverage of the same boundaries comes from CS-TYPES8 integer-edges and CS-RANGE-OUT.
308:**Wire exactness (RULINGS Q30).**
312:| WR-JSON | a JSON value containing -0, a non-finite number, or a number that is not exactly a binary64 value is rejected at every qualified write route with E-INEXACT-JSON-NUMBER (InvalidTripleError / invalid-value, detail inexact-json-number), never normalized; the guard runs before CanonicalJson, whose canonical identity is unchanged; approximateNumbers: reject applies recursively inside List and Struct | RULINGS Q30a | WIRE-JSON-NEGZERO, WIRE-JSON-NONFINITE, WIRE-JSON-INEXACT, WIRE-JSON-NESTED-LIST, WIRE-JSON-NESTED-STRUCT, WIRE-JSON-BEFORE-CANONICAL |
313:| WR-INSTANT | an instant given as -0 is rejected with E-NEGATIVE-ZERO-INSTANT (NoNegativeZero), never turned into 0 | RULINGS Q30b | WIRE-INSTANT-NEGZERO |
314:| WR-X-ENCODINGS | ProjectionSpec pins the XTDB encodings F02 proved exact: card-many DOUBLE and integer lists are written as ARRAY[CAST(? AS <type>) ...], never as one JSON parameter; date and instant are read as text, never through the driver's TIMESTAMPTZ decoding; any other encoding fails the projection check | RULINGS Q30c | WIRE-X-DOUBLE-LIST-ARRAY, WIRE-X-INTEGER-LIST-ARRAY, WIRE-X-DATE-TEXT, WIRE-X-INSTANT-TEXT, WIRE-X-OTHER-ENCODING-FAILS |
315:| WR-Q-SHADOW | a QLever binary64 shadow counts as exact only if it is not -0 and parsing its %.13g rendering returns the same bits; integers only within +/-2^59; otherwise the lexical channel is authoritative and the shadow is flagged inexact (coarse pruning only, never an answer) | RULINGS Q30d | WIRE-Q-SHADOW-NEGZERO, WIRE-Q-SHADOW-13G, WIRE-Q-SHADOW-INT59, WIRE-Q-LEXICAL-AUTHORITATIVE |
316:| WR-INTEGER-RANGE | the ProjectionSpec integer range rule is narrowed to its safe-integer domain [-(2^53-1), 2^53-1] (cosmetic) | RULINGS Q30e | WIRE-INTEGER-SAFE-RANGE |
387:set semantics: equal values collapse to one element; elements ordered first by type family, then by value. Family order: boolean, integer, number, decimal, datetime, string, ref, json, blob. boolean: false < true; integer: numeric; number: numeric IEEE (-0.0 and +0.0 collapse, CMP-NEGZERO); decimal: exact numeric value (1.50 and 1.5 are one value); datetime: instant; string: Unicode codepoint sequence; ref: codepoint sequence of the target id; json: codepoint sequence of the canonical JSON text; blob: codepoint sequence of the content id. Family order per RULINGS Q14a and Q18: decimal is its own family between number and datetime; inside it, values sort by exact numeric value.
433:| E-INEXACT-JSON-NUMBER | InvalidTripleError | invalid-value | preflight | existing reason with detail inexact-json-number (RULINGS Q30a): -0, a non-finite number or an inexact binary64 inside a JSON value, at any depth |
434:| E-NEGATIVE-ZERO-INSTANT | InvalidTripleError | invalid-value | preflight | existing reason with detail no-negative-zero (RULINGS Q30b): an instant given as -0 |
