1299:- Statement: Every lane accepts every integer in [-(2^53-1), 2^53-1]; on every qualified write route (the Triplex writer, the bulk import lane, replay and restore: RANGE-OUT-WRITER, RANGE-OUT-BULK, RANGE-OUT-REPLAY, RANGE-OUT-RESTORE) every CS-RANGE-OUT case (integers beyond the lane's declared LANE_INTEGER_RANGE, for QLever beyond +/-2^59) fails atomically with E-VALUE-OUT-OF-RANGE before any mutation, with no residue on any access path, and is never wrapped, widened or rounded. Bare native wire access is a diagnostic surface, not a qualified route: QLever's native wrap there is matrix row 51 (KNOWN-LIMIT KL-Q1) and diagnostic D-Q-INT60, never a failure of this obligation (RULINGS Q23).
1318:**`G1.wire.write-guards`: Qualified write routes reject inexact JSON numbers and -0 instants.**
1319:- Statement: Per WIRE_RULES WR-JSON, WR-INSTANT and WR-INTEGER-RANGE (RULINGS Q30a, Q30b, Q30e): on every qualified write route a JSON value holding -0, a non-finite number or an inexact binary64 at any depth, including inside List and Struct, fails atomically with E-INEXACT-JSON-NUMBER before CanonicalJson runs and before any mutation; an instant given as -0 fails with E-NEGATIVE-ZERO-INSTANT; the ProjectionSpec integer range rule covers only the safe-integer domain.
1321:- Cases: CS-WIRE (json, instant, integer-range, TO-AUTHOR); CS-WIRE (WIRE-JSON-NEGZERO, WIRE-JSON-NONFINITE, WIRE-JSON-INEXACT, WIRE-JSON-NESTED-LIST, WIRE-JSON-NESTED-STRUCT, WIRE-JSON-BEFORE-CANONICAL, WIRE-INSTANT-NEGZERO, WIRE-INTEGER-SAFE-RANGE, TO-AUTHOR).
1324:- Mutation controls: M-JSON-NEGZERO-NORMALIZE.
1325:- Sources: RULINGS Q30; EXP-F02 wire-exactness report.
1328:**`G1.wire.xtdb-encodings`: XTDB projections use the pinned exact encodings.**
1329:- Statement: Per WR-X-ENCODINGS (RULINGS Q30c): card-many DOUBLE and integer lists are written as ARRAY[CAST(? AS <type>) ...] and read back bit-exact (-0 kept); date and instant are read as text and keep year 0001 and microseconds; any other encoding fails the projection check (WIRE-X-OTHER-ENCODING-FAILS).
1334:- Mutation controls: M-X-JSON-LIST-PARAM.
1335:- Sources: RULINGS Q30c; EXP-F02 wire-exactness report findings 2 and 4.
1338:**`G1.wire.qlever-shadow`: QLever numeric shadows count as exact only when they survive export.**
1339:- Statement: Per WR-Q-SHADOW (RULINGS Q30d): a binary64 shadow is exact only if it is not -0 and its %.13g rendering parses back to the same bits, and integer shadows only within +/-2^59; otherwise the lexical channel is authoritative and the shadow is flagged inexact and never used for an answer (WIRE-Q-LEXICAL-AUTHORITATIVE).
1345:- Sources: RULINGS Q30d; EXP-F02 wire-exactness report finding 5.
1494:### Goal 3. Useful plans, costing, preconstruction admission, recursion
1515:**`G3.admission.preconstruction`: Expensive queries are refused before construction.**
1630:- Statement: For every declared projection whose definition aggregates over many subjects, and every CS-NUMERIC projection-sums case, the engine-side SUM returns the AG-SUM-I total (exact; E-INTEGER-OVERFLOW only for a final total outside the integer range), using on XTDB SUM(CAST(x AS DECIMAL(32,0))) once EXP-X02 confirms it, else the split-sum fallback (RULINGS Q13, Q19), with EXP-F02 cross-checking the chosen recipe; whichever recipe is used passes these same cases; unconverted integer AVG fails with E-INTEGER-AVG; per-subject sums run in the kernel and are covered by G2.arith.integer-exact.
1748:**`G7.journal.logical-contract`: The logical journal stays queryable (C6).**
1845:- Statement: Per COMPOSITION ackOwnership (RULINGS Q31e, replacing Q28's ACK clause): the source of truth sends every ACK after its own publication (CO-ACK-AFTER-SOURCE-PUBLICATION, OBS-ACK-ORDER); the pull at the returned position is served by an index eligible for it, else by the fallback, so read-your-own-write is exact (CO-RYOW-ROUTED); during a current-index outage commits and ACKs continue and pulls fall back (CO-OUTAGE-ACKS-CONTINUE), and the index catches up from the feed through a bounded queue (CO-CATCHUP-BOUNDED-QUEUE).
1847:- Cases: CS-COMPOSITION (ack, TO-AUTHOR); CS-COMPOSITION (CO-ACK-AFTER-SOURCE-PUBLICATION, CO-RYOW-ROUTED, CO-OUTAGE-ACKS-CONTINUE, CO-CATCHUP-BOUNDED-QUEUE, TO-AUTHOR).
1854:**`G7.position.coordinates`: Positions are totally ordered coordinates with a fully resolved watermark.**
1855:- Statement: Per POSITION: a position is (stream, epoch, offset, ordinal), ordered lexicographically and not necessarily dense (PO-LEXICOGRAPHIC-ORDER); several Triplex transacts in one engine transaction take consecutive ordinals at one offset, assigned atomically with their assertions and the subject compare-and-set; a watermark is a fully resolved prefix and every aborted offset is listed in the feed's resolution stream (PO-ABORTED-LISTED); O-REPLAY sorts by coordinate and assigns its own dense ordinals for cuts (PO-ORACLE-DENSE-ORDINALS). The required selection depends on the composition (position_selection, RULINGS Q32e): a dense-position composition (the A0 controls on today's scope-counter adapters) closes with the nested-cut cases PO-NESTED-CUTS-DENSE, PO-LEXICOGRAPHIC-ORDER and PO-ORACLE-DENSE-ORDINALS; a non-dense one also needs the four falsifiers FX-NESTED-CUTS, FX-ABORT-GAP, FX-CRASH-APPEND-RESOLUTION and FX-RESUME-AT-ABORT-GAP before any comparative claim (GATE-POSITION).
1857:- Cases: CS-POSITION (all, TO-AUTHOR); CS-POSITION (FX-NESTED-CUTS, FX-ABORT-GAP, FX-CRASH-APPEND-RESOLUTION, FX-RESUME-AT-ABORT-GAP, PO-LEXICOGRAPHIC-ORDER, PO-ORACLE-DENSE-ORDINALS, PO-ABORTED-LISTED, PO-NESTED-CUTS-DENSE, TO-AUTHOR).
