   263	| Scenario | Added schedule | Expected outcome |
   264	| --- | --- | --- |
   265	| GS.03 | A vendor's older revision of a status arrives after a newer one for the same subject | Acknowledged as `superseded`: no change to current values, recorded in the journal (provisional, flagged for Sam) |
   266	| GS.04b | The current index's feed consumer is cut for 60 s while the source keeps committing | Reads at bases beyond the index watermark wait or route to a fallback per section 4.1; none answers from the stale index |
   282	4. **Derived channels count.** An order key reveals ordering, so a PII attribute's order key is either inside the encrypted envelope (range
   283	   rules on PII then run in the kernel after decryption) or the attribute is not eligible for range rules.
   284	5. **Restore re-applies erasure.** The erasure ledger is kept apart from the data and replayed after any restore or rebuild.
   285	6. **Tests:** a reader already pinned when the erasure lands; a rebuild after erasure; a restore from a pre-erasure backup; independent
   286	   post-erasure expected answers from the oracle.
   287	7. **A current erasure frontier, independent of the requested basis (Codex round 2, finding 17).** An index or cache may serve any basis
   288	   only after it has applied every erasure published so far; an old-basis read never passes on the strength of its basis alone. Erasure
   289	   invalidates every old-basis cache entry that holds the erased value.
   290	8. **"Modulo erasure" applies to every reproduction contract:** the C6 whole-journal comparison, raw and native query results, SDK
   291	   results, cursors and explain. Both sides of a comparison replace erased values with their markers before comparing.
   292	9. **Key-destruction evidence:** for crypto-shredding, the lane's inventory must show that every surviving copy of a protected value
   293	   (and of anything derived from it) is encrypted only under the destroyed key; deleting some key is not evidence.

