   166	
   167	1. **A composition names four assignments:** source of truth, current index, history index, analytical copy, each XTDB or QLever.
   168	2. **Receipts bind every component:** engine image digest, configuration hash and slot per assignment, plus every replication edge (feed
   169	   consumer source hash, translation generation) and each index's watermark at read time.
   170	3. **Coordinates.** A position is (stream, epoch, offset, ordinal): the source of truth's log stream and epoch, the engine's log offset (or
   171	   the broker's log frame for QLever) and the ordinal of a Triplex transact inside that engine transaction, ordered lexicographically.
   172	   Positions need not be dense. A watermark is a fully resolved prefix: every position at or below it is either applied or known aborted,
   173	   which is stronger than "the greatest position seen". Epoch changes are explicit: a watermark from one epoch never satisfies a basis in
   174	   another without the mapping C1 defines.
   175	4. **Eligibility to serve a basis B** needs all of: the index's watermark has reached B; C1's eligibility for the query (mapping,
   176	   valid time, exact cut); and the index can actually reconstruct B. A current-only index can reconstruct B only when B is at or after the
   177	   last change to every attribute the read touches (the validity interval of R18), otherwise it is not eligible however far its watermark
   178	   has advanced. Erasure adds a second frontier: the index must also have applied every erasure published so far, independently of B
   179	   (section 7 item 7).
   180	5. **Fallback is deadline-aware.** A read that finds no eligible index waits at most 10 ms for one, then routes to a fallback that is
   181	   eligible (the source of truth's own query surface or another index). The fallback's latency counts in the read's own latency, the
   182	   receipt records the route and the surface language (a cross-language fallback is counted per surface), and fallbacks are reported as a
   183	   share of reads per window. No index ever answers for a basis it is not eligible for.
   184	6. **ACK ownership (revised after Codex round 2, finding 16).** The source of truth sends the ACK after its own publication. Read-your-own-
   185	   write holds by routing: the pull at the returned position is served by an index eligible for it, else by the fallback. The current
   186	   index is kept synchronous with commits in normal operation, so the fast path serves that pull; when the current index is down or behind
   187	   (GS.04b), commits and ACKs continue, pulls fall back, the current index catches up from the feed through a bounded queue, and the
   188	   receipt measures fallback share and recovery time. History and analytics stay asynchronous behind watermarks.
   189	7. **Layers are swapped one at a time** under matched workloads at small rungs (current-index candidates fed the same feed and measured on
   190	   P1 and P4; history candidates on past-basis reads and GS.12; source candidates on P2 and P3). Only the two or three best compositions
   191	   go on to end-to-end validation with the golden scenarios.
