  2326	**`NS.P1`: P1: many concurrent pinned reads that never block writers (per surface).**
  2327	- Statement: With the frozen writer trace running, closed-loop readers at 1, 4, 16, 64 and 256 (and 1,024 stress) each name their basis (valid time and record time or tx), in the lane's language, under TARGETS 1.1 P1: readers and writers share one hot set (at least half the reads target subjects written in the previous 60 s, NS-P1-HOT-SET); 10% of subgraph reads are split into two or more queries at the same named basis, scheduled to straddle a command the frozen trace already holds for that subject (step 1 before its ACK, step 2 after), and must equal the oracle at the requested basis (NS-P1-SPLIT-READ); the driver adds no writes of its own, so faster readers never change the write load (NS-P1-SPLIT-NO-DRIVER-WRITES, RULINGS Q31b); every window contains at least one maintenance event (NS-P1-MAINTENANCE-IN-WINDOW); the driver records the requested basis itself and the verifier checks answers at it (NS-P1-DRIVER-BASIS); reader-caused writer waits are attributed by matched-phase comparison against the zero-reader run and by engine-side wait evidence where the engine exposes it (NS-P1-WAIT-ATTRIBUTION). Every NS-P1-* bar holds: zero violations, goodput at 256 not below 64, read p99 within NS-P1-READ-P99-256-MS, writer commit p99 within NS-P1-WRITER-P99-RATIO-256 of its zero-reader baseline, no command waits beyond NS-P1-WRITER-WAIT-MAX-MS, and at 1,024 goodput within NS-P1-GOODPUT-1024-VS-256, writer p99 within NS-P1-WRITER-P99-RATIO-1024, back to the 256-level bar within NS-P1-RECOVERY-MS.
  2328	- Lanes: all three; receipt scope lane. Verifier mode: re-evaluate-and-spot.
  2329	- Cases: CS-NS (p1-ladder, p1-stress, COMPILED); CS-NS (NS-P1-HOT-SET, NS-P1-SPLIT-READ, NS-P1-SPLIT-NO-DRIVER-WRITES, NS-P1-MAINTENANCE-IN-WINDOW, NS-P1-DRIVER-BASIS, NS-P1-WAIT-ATTRIBUTION, COMPILED).
